  "question": "Background / Context\nThe Happy Phone Company is a fictional business specializing in retailing mobile phones. To enhance the management of its operations, the company requires a robust database system that handles data related to employees, customers, suppliers, phones, and contracts.\n\nObjectives\nThe main objective is to develop a comprehensive database system that efficiently manages and stores essential information regarding:\n- Employees and their roles within the company.\n- Customer identities and purchase histories.\n- Suppliers and the details of their supply activities.\n- Inventory of mobile phones, including their make, model, and stock details.\n- Contracts associated with phone sales.\n\nStakeholders & Users\n- Stakeholders: Company Management, IT Department.\n- Primary Users: Employees handling sales and inventory, and management personnel overseeing operations.\n\nFunctional Descriptions\n1. Employee Management:  \n   Stores data on employees, including a unique employee ID, name, and position.\n\n2. Customer Management:  \n   Captures customer details with a customer ID and name.\n\n3. Supplier Management:  \n   Maintains supplier information, including a unique name and contact phone number.\n\n4. Phone Inventory Management:  \n   Tracks phones by product code, including details on make, model, retail price, and stock quantity.\n\n5. Supply Chain Management:  \n   Manages supplier relations, capturing the most recent supply details, including supply price, quantity, and date.\n\n6. Sales and Purchase Management:  \n   Records purchases made by customers, including purchase date and contract details if applicable.\n\n7. Contract Management:  \n   Oversees contracts detailing contract numbers, terms, and pricing related to sales.\n\nNon-Functional Descriptions\n- Ensure data integrity and consistency, particularly referring to relationships between entities like customer purchases, phone inventories, and supplier data.\n- Support ease of access and quick retrieval of information to facilitate sales and inventory management operations.\n- Provide scalability to accommodate the future growth of business operations and data volume.",
  "ddl": "-- Schema for database: schemabench_0186\nCREATE TABLE `employees` (\n  `employee_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `employee_name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `position` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`employee_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `customers` (\n  `customer_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `customer_name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`customer_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `suppliers` (\n  `supplier_name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `contact_phone` varchar(20) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`supplier_name`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `phones` (\n  `product_code` varchar(50) NOT NULL,\n  `make` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `model` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `retail_price` decimal(10,2) NOT NULL,\n  `stock_quantity` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`product_code`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `supplier_supply` (\n  `supply_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `supplier_name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `product_code` varchar(50) NOT NULL,\n  `supply_price` decimal(10,2) NOT NULL,\n  `quantity` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `supply_date` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`supply_id`),\n  KEY `supplier_supply_ibfk_1` (`supplier_name`),\n  KEY `supplier_supply_ibfk_2` (`product_code`),\n  CONSTRAINT `supplier_supply_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`supplier_name`) REFERENCES `suppliers` (`supplier_name`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `supplier_supply_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`product_code`) REFERENCES `phones` (`product_code`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `contracts` (\n  `contract_number` varchar(50) NOT NULL,\n  `terms` text NOT NULL,\n  `pricing` decimal(10,2) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`contract_number`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `purchases` (\n  `purchase_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `customer_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `employee_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `product_code` varchar(50) NOT NULL,\n  `contract_number` varchar(50) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `purchase_date` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`purchase_id`),\n  KEY `purchases_ibfk_1` (`customer_id`),\n  KEY `purchases_ibfk_2` (`employee_id`),\n  KEY `purchases_ibfk_3` (`product_code`),\n  KEY `purchases_ibfk_4` (`contract_number`),\n  CONSTRAINT `purchases_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`customer_id`) REFERENCES `customers` (`customer_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `purchases_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`employee_id`) REFERENCES `employees` (`employee_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `purchases_ibfk_3` FOREIGN KEY (`product_code`) REFERENCES `phones` (`product_code`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `purchases_ibfk_4` FOREIGN KEY (`contract_number`) REFERENCES `contracts` (`contract_number`) ON DELETE SET NULL ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;",
